<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Expo '92</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Seville Expo '92</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Expo '92 was a world's fair held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, from 20 April to 12 October 1992, marking the 500th anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s first voyage to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Built on the island of La Cartuja, it received more than 40 million visits and brought major investment, including Spain's first high-speed railway, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and Seville.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Universal Exhibition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> took as its theme The Age of Discoveries. It was first meant to be held jointly with Chicago, but the Chicago 1992 World's Fair was cancelled over national, state and local funding difficulties, and the smaller Genoa Expo '92, a specialised exhibition in memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>, ran at the same time. The commissioner-general was Manuel Olivencia. Besides the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>–Seville line, whose AVE service opened on 21 April 1992 with a stop at the exhibition site, Seville gained a renovated airport, the SE-30 ring road with the Centenario Bridge, and five new bridges over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a>, among them the Alamillo and the Barqueta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>More than 100 countries took part on a site of 215 hectares, which took several days to see and was crossed by bus, ferry boat, cable car and monorail. Among the pavilions were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s, the largest wooden structure in the world, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>'s re-creation of a palace and the cube and sphere of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s; the pavilions of Spain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> drew the most visitors. A Plaza de América let every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> nation take part for the first time, and a Plaza de África did the same for developing African countries. Each day honoured a different country, and evenings closed with light, water and fireworks on the Lake of Spain.</p><p>The site stayed open until 4 in the morning. Against the Sevillian summer, a system of water mist cooled the avenues, its Bioclimatic Sphere becoming an emblem of the fair, and the mascot, a white bird named Curro, was designed by Heinz Edelmann.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the fair many pavilions were demolished or moved, and others became part of the Cartuja Science and Technology Park, while city services took over further buildings. A theme park opened on the Lake of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and reopened as Isla Mágica on 28 June 1997; in 1998 the remaining regional pavilions, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>'s, were demolished to extend it, and the Agua Mágica water park followed in 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Expo '92” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The 1992 Barcelona Olympics and <b>Seville Expo</b> marked modernisation.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-spain">Kingdom of Spain</a></li></ul></section>
